course: medicine
lesson: 56
title: Sleep for growing and for playing well
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sleep isn't a break from training, it is the part of training where the body does its building: muscles repair the tiny strains of the day, the brain stores the new skills, and growth hormone is let out in deep sleep.
explain: Liam in Dublin wants the late boxing before his cup final, «Ah sure I'll sleep when I'm dead, I'm grand» («grand» is Irish for fine), but his mam's telly-off at nine gives him about 10 hours, right in the 9 to 12 hours a child of 6 to 12 needs.
explain: Callum, a keeper from Newcastle, «kipped» (slept) six hours and says «Howay man, I'm buzzing» (Geordie for come on, I'm full of energy), then dives late three times, because a tired brain is slow before tired legs are.
explain: Short nights add up like a debt, a big Saturday lie-in can't pay a whole week back, and late cola, long afternoon naps and «no rest days» all steal from the sleep that turns practice into fitness.
fact: Children aged 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night, more than most adults, who need about 7 to 9.
fact: During sleep the body repairs used muscles and the brain sorts and stores what happened in the day, including new skills.
fact: Most growth hormone is released in deep sleep, mostly early in the night; sleep helps you reach the height your genes allow, but it can't make you taller than that.
fact: One sleep cycle lasts about 90 minutes, and a night has several of them.
fact: One bad night before a big game usually does little harm, because excitement helps on the day; a whole week of short nights hurts much more.
fact: Missed sleep adds up as sleep debt; a weekend lie-in helps a little but can't repay a whole week and makes Sunday night harder.
fact: A tired brain reacts more slowly and makes worse choices, which keepers, goalies and batters notice first.
fact: In a study reported from Stanford, college basketball players who slept longer for several weeks shot free throws more accurately.
fact: Studies of young athletes reported more injuries in those who slept less than about 8 hours.
fact: A short nap of about 20 minutes early in the afternoon can refresh you; a long late nap makes night sleep harder.
fact: For early training you need an early bedtime: count back from your alarm the hours you need.
fact: Caffeine in cola, tea and energy drinks blocks sleepiness, and about half of it is still in the body about 5 hours later.
fact: Muscles get stronger while they repair during rest, so rest days are part of a training plan.
fact: «No cap» is young slang for no lie, «sus» means suspicious, and «mid» means boring or so-so.
exercise: Liam: «I'll sleep when I'm dead, I'm grand» before his final. What's true about sleep? | It's when training turns into fitness | It's wasted time a player could train | It only matters for babies | It's only needed after a match
exercise: How much sleep does a child of 6 to 12 need a night? | About 9 to 12 hours | About 5 to 6 hours | About 7 hours at most | About 15 to 18 hours
exercise: Bed at 9 pm, up at 7 am. How long is that? | 10 hours | 8 hours | 9 hours | 11 hours
exercise: Priya: «sleep fifteen hours and I'll be massive, no cap». Is she right? | No, genes mostly decide height | Yes, more sleep means more height | Yes, but only on weekends | No, sleep stops you growing
exercise: When is most growth hormone released? | In deep sleep, early in the night | Straight after breakfast | While you are running | Only on the day of a match
exercise: Ava can't sleep the night before her grand final: «I'm cooked». What matters most? | The sleep earlier in the week | Only that very last night | How long she lies in after | Nothing, sleep doesn't matter
exercise: Jayden needs 10 hours and gets 7 for 5 school nights. How much is missed? | 15 hours | 3 hours | 10 hours | 35 hours
exercise: Can one long Saturday lie-in repay a week of short nights? | No, and it upsets Sunday night | Yes, completely and all at once | Yes, if he sleeps till 3 pm | No, lie-ins make you shorter
exercise: Callum kipped 6 hours and dives late. What did tiredness slow first? | His brain and reactions | Only his legs, which felt heavy | Only his gloves, which felt slippy | Nothing, he was buzzing and sharp
exercise: Zara scrolls late after shooting practice: «less sleep, that's the grind». What does sleep do for practice? | The brain stores the skill while you sleep | It wipes out what you practised | It does nothing for skill | It only rests your eyes
exercise: Thabo sleeps late every night and hurts his ankle again. What did studies report? | Short sleepers got injured more often | Short sleepers never got injured | Sleep has no link with injury | Only adults get injured from tiredness
exercise: Ella naps three hours after lunch and is wide awake at midnight. Why? | A long nap used up her sleepiness | Tennis keeps you awake all night | Naps make your body clock faster | She didn't nap long enough to feel rested
exercise: Oliver's alarm is 5:30 am for swimming and he needs 10 hours. When's bedtime? | 7:30 pm | 9:30 pm | 10:30 pm | 5:30 pm
exercise: Sienna drinks cola at 6 pm. Roughly how much caffeine is still working at 11 pm? | About half | None at all | All of it | About double
exercise: Arjun trains every day: «rest days are for the lazy». What happens without rest? | Muscles never fully repair | Muscles grow fastest without a break | Legs get lighter every day | Nothing, rest is optional
exercise: What is a good nap before an evening game? | A short one, about 20 minutes, early | Three hours just before dinner | No sleep at all for two days | A nap that lasts till midnight
test: Jayden in Ohio: «I'll just catch it all up Saturday, bro, easy». What's the snag? | Debt that big can't be repaid in one lie-in | Saturday sleep counts double | He can store sleep for later | Weekends have extra hours
test: Callum's dad groans «bloody hell, son» after a late dive. Best fix for next week? | An earlier bedtime all week | New, bigger gloves for his hands | Two energy drinks before kick-off | Running extra laps at midnight
test: Mia's big sister: «Sleep's a waste, pros train instead». What would a sports scientist say? | Sleep is part of training | Pros never sleep more than 5 hours | Training late at night works better | Only lazy players bother with sleep
test: What would happen if Sienna drank water instead of cola before her game? | She'd find it easier to fall asleep | She'd be wide awake till 3 am anyway | She'd play much worse | She'd need a 3-hour nap
test: A cartoon hero stays up all week, then wins the final with a perfect save. Real life? | Tired keepers react more slowly | Less sleep makes keepers sharper | Cartoon keepers are always right | Keepers need no sleep at all
test: Oliver: «lights out at half seven? that's for babies». Coach's real reason? | His body needs 10 hours before 5:30 | Babies swim faster than him | The pool closes at half seven | Lights cost far too much after eight o'clock
test: Priya's brother: «that's well sus». Which bit is right in Priya's plan? | Sleeping well lets her grow as planned | Sleeping 15 hours makes her massive | Sleep stretches the bones | Naps add a centimetre each
test: Ava, still awake at 2 am before her final, panics. What's the calm truth? | One rough night rarely ruins a game | She has lost the match already | She must play without sleeping again | Her body can't play after one bad night
test: Thabo: «Ag, sleep is for the weak, bru» («bru» is South African for bro). Which is true? | Tough players need their sleep too | Weak players are the ones who need less sleep | Sleep makes ankles weaker | Only goalkeepers need sleep
test: In a Stanford study reported on sleep, what improved when players slept longer? | Their free-throw accuracy | Their height, by two centimetres | Their shoe size and their foot speed | Nothing at all, it made them slower
test: Ella says her day was «so mid» after the long nap. What does «mid» mean? | So-so or boring | Brilliant and exciting | Very tired and ill | Halfway through a match
exam: Identify the quantity of nightly sleep recommended for a child between six and twelve years of age. | Approximately nine to twelve hours | Approximately five to six hours | Approximately three to four hours | Approximately fifteen to eighteen hours
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between sleep and athletic training? | Sleep is the period in which the benefits of training are consolidated | Sleep is a period in which training is undone | Sleep is of no consequence to athletic performance | Sleep is a substitute for all training
exam: It is given that a participant requires ten hours of sleep and must rise at half past five. Determine the appropriate hour of retiring. | Half past seven in the evening | Half past nine in the evening | Half past eleven in the evening | Midnight precisely
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which caffeinated beverages are inadvisable in the evening. | Caffeine suppresses sleepiness for several hours | Caffeine induces immediate drowsiness | Caffeine has no effect after one minute | Caffeine replaces the need for sleep
